.. raw:: html

    <div class="sphx-glr-thumbcontainer" tooltip="A billiard particle moves in a straight line at constant speed inside a closed boundary and undergoes specular reflection, \mathbf{v}&#x27; = \mathbf{v} - 2(\mathbf{v}\cdot\mathbf{n})\,\mathbf{n}, whenever it strikes the boundary. Here the boundary is the rectangle |x| \le w/2,\ |y| \le h/2 of width w and height h, whose walls are all axis-aligned, so \mathbf{n} is always \pm\hat{x} or \pm\hat{y} -- reflections off the horizontal and vertical walls therefore only ever flip the sign of one velocity component. The rectangular billiard is integrable: each individual ray only ever visits a handful of discrete \sin\phi values (the sine of the angle between the outgoing velocity and the local boundary tangent). This example plots a single trajectory and a Poincare section built from several rays launched at different angles.">

.. only:: html

  .. image:: /api/gallery/chaos/billiards/images/thumb/sphx_glr_plot_rectangle_billiard_thumb.gif
